WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:21.000 --> 00:00:24.800
Elizabeth Economy: Maritime disputes in the
East and South China Seas are a pressing matter

00:00:24.890 --> 00:00:30.669
for the United States, China, and much of
the rest of the world. It is a region that

00:00:30.669 --> 00:00:36.489
is rich in natural resources; home to many
of the world's most dynamic economies; and

00:00:36.489 --> 00:00:41.850
an important global trade route for the energy
supplies and other goods. It is also a region

00:00:41.850 --> 00:00:46.000
in which power politics are at play and defense
budgets are rising rapidly.

00:00:49.000 --> 00:00:54.910
China claims roughly 90 percent of the total area of the South China Sea. This area includes the Paracels

00:00:54.910 --> 00:00:59.780
and Spratly islands chains, which both Vietnam
and the Philippines lay claim to. In addition,

00:00:59.780 --> 00:01:04.150
the Philippines and China both lay claim to
the Scarborough Shoal. Malaysia and Brunei

00:01:04.150 --> 00:01:07.799
also claim maritime territory in the South
China Sea.

00:01:07.799 --> 00:01:12.060
A similar dispute takes place in the East
China Sea, which encompasses the Diaoyu or

00:01:12.060 --> 00:01:15.290
Senkaku Islands, which are claimed by China,
Taiwan, and Japan.

00:01:17.000 --> 00:01:22.150
Xi Jinping, Vice President of China: We are
strongly committed to safeguarding the country's

00:01:22.150 --> 00:01:26.909
sovereignty and security, and defending our
territorial integrity. We are committed to

00:01:26.909 --> 00:01:30.830
resolving differences with neighbours concerning
territorial land, territorial sea, and maritime

00:01:30.830 --> 00:01:33.320
rights and interests, peacefully through friendly
negotiations.

00:01:33.320 --> 00:01:37.750
Richard Haas: After decades in which the history
of this part of the world has been dominated

00:01:37.750 --> 00:01:42.330
by economic issues, we're beginning to see
the re-emergence of geopolitical concerns,

00:01:42.330 --> 00:01:45.000
of competition, of nationalism.

00:01:51.000 --> 00:01:58.000
And the real question is: can the diplomacy of the Asia Pacific region handle these new pressures?

00:01:58.000 --> 00:02:04.000
If we begin to see the tectonic plates really moving, the danger is that twenty-first century Asia

00:02:05.000 --> 00:02:07.000
could begin to go the way of twentieth century Europe.

00:02:21.000 --> 00:02:24.629
Shen Dingli: South China Sea and East China
Sea, they are different. For East China Sea

00:02:24.629 --> 00:02:31.169
it's more political. China considers that
we have been invaded by Japan and Japan stole

00:02:31.169 --> 00:02:38.169
our island. But for South China Sea, largely
it's about economics: fishery and seabed.

00:02:38.489 --> 00:02:44.879
It has something to do with China's sustainable
development. So, economics empowers China's

00:02:44.879 --> 00:02:45.879
international status.

00:02:47.000 --> 00:02:48.930
Benigno Aquino III, Philippine President:
We're not going to engage in an arms race

00:02:48.930 --> 00:02:55.930
with them. We are not going to escalate the
tensions there but we do have to protect our

00:02:56.019 --> 00:03:01.120
rights and that has to be very very clear,
we will not be pushed around because we are

00:03:01.120 --> 00:03:02.590
a tiny state compared with theirs.

00:03:02.590 --> 00:03:08.389
Sheila Smith: Japan is a very powerful country
and yet even it worries about China's rise.

00:03:08.389 --> 00:03:15.389
It is increasingly worried about Chinese willingness
to solve some of these problems together with

00:03:15.400 --> 00:03:16.499
Japan through diplomatic compromise.

00:03:16.499 --> 00:03:18.510
Shinzo Abe, Japanese Prime Minister: The environment
surrounding our nation is getting more severe.

00:03:18.510 --> 00:03:20.079
We will take every measure to promote and
manage and we will give our best efforts to

00:03:20.079 --> 00:03:27.079
guard the remote islands at the borders. Under
this administration, we vow to protect our

00:03:28.049 --> 00:03:35.049
nationals' lives, treasure, territorial land,
territorial waters and territorial skies.

00:03:36.000 --> 00:03:41.329
Sheila Smith: This escalatory path is very
recent. It's changed in large part because

00:03:41.329 --> 00:03:48.329
of popular sensitivities to the sovereignty
dispute, some political opportunism on the

00:03:49.639 --> 00:03:56.430
part of political leaders. But also now you
see the Chinese maritime interests in the

00:03:56.430 --> 00:03:58.939
region, are becoming much more active.

00:04:01.000 --> 00:04:05.939
Simon Tay: I think at the heart of these issues
are Chinese nationalism and a sense of wounding

00:04:07.180 --> 00:04:13.809
from the past, and the projection forward
that as it rises economically, there is a

00:04:13.809 --> 00:04:19.660
larger place for China in the world and the
immediate region around them. But it's not

00:04:19.660 --> 00:04:24.840
really accepted by many others in Asia. So
I think this is creating the overall climate

00:04:24.840 --> 00:04:28.480
of distrust in which these tensions
at sea -- these tensions at sea become much

00:04:28.480 --> 00:04:32.250
more than just about little rocks.

00:04:35.000 --> 00:04:39.250
Barack Obama: The United States is turning
our attention to the vast potential of the

00:04:39.570 --> 00:04:44.750
Asia Pacific region. The Asia Pacific is critical
to achieving my highest priority, and that's

00:04:44.750 --> 00:04:47.280
creating jobs and opportunity for the American
people.

00:04:47.280 --> 00:04:52.150
Elizabeth Economy: I think the United States
was eager to re-engage with a region that

00:04:52.150 --> 00:04:58.200
was so economically dynamic. And at the same
time, I think the pivot was really a response

00:04:58.200 --> 00:05:05.110
to others in the region who called for the
United States to reassert its presence in

00:05:05.110 --> 00:05:09.870
the Asia-Pacific because they were concerned
about China's rise.

00:05:12.000 --> 00:05:16.870
Richard Haass: The dilemma facing U.S. foreign
policy is nothing short of exquisite. What

00:05:17.440 --> 00:05:21.090
we have to do is be sufficiently supportive
of our friends and allies so they don't go

00:05:21.090 --> 00:05:26.250
their own way. But we can't be so supportive
of them to give them a blank check so to speak

00:05:26.250 --> 00:05:30.410
so they actually then begin to act recklessly.

00:05:36.000 --> 00:05:39.600
Elizabeth Economy: My greatest fear is that
the small mishap is going to blow up into

00:05:39.670 --> 00:05:44.540
something much bigger because of the distrust,
because of the lack of shared rules of the

00:05:44.540 --> 00:05:49.230
road, and the inability to define the territorial boundaries.

00:05:53.000 --> 00:05:57.700
Sheila Smith: If there is a use of force between
Japan and China, this could be

00:05:57.750 --> 00:06:03.740
all out conflict between these two Asian giants.
And as a treaty ally of Japan, will automatically

00:06:03.740 --> 00:06:05.210
involve the United States.

00:06:06.400 --> 00:06:09.590
Elizabeth Economy: There are a number of preventive
measures that have been discussed for a number

00:06:09.590 --> 00:06:16.590
of years: the joint sharing of resources,
fisheries or oil and gas, joint military exercises,

00:06:17.080 --> 00:06:24.080
a multilateral binding code of conduct. And
then there's international arbitration.

00:06:27.700 --> 00:06:34.830
These maritime disputes suggest that China's rise
is not going to be without its frictions,

00:06:34.830 --> 00:06:39.750
that in many instances China feels that its
economic throw weight really does give it

00:06:39.750 --> 00:06:45.240
a greater stance and a greater ability to
assert its interests, in some cases to reform

00:06:45.240 --> 00:06:50.780
norms, and in some cases to upend them. And
I think that it's going to require a lot of

00:06:50.780 --> 00:06:56.400
U.S. diplomatic skill as well as efforts by
others within the region to maintain their

00:06:56.400 --> 00:07:00.000
own interests as well as to find room to cooperate
with China.

